---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const serviceSchema = {
  '@type': 'Service',
  '@id': 'https://brightersites.app/therapist-websites/#service',
  name: 'Website Design for Therapists and Counselors',
  serviceType: 'Mental Health Web Design',
  url: 'https://brightersites.app/therapist-websites/',
  provider: {
    '@id': 'https://brightersites.app/#business',
  },
  areaServed: {
    '@type': 'Country',
    name: 'United States',
  },
  audience: {
    '@type': 'Audience',
    audienceType:
      'Therapists, counselors, psychologists, clinical social workers, and behavioral health practices',
  },
};
---

<Layout
  title="Custom Website Design for Therapists & Counselors | Brighter Sites"
  description="Custom website design for therapists, counselors, psychologists, and private practices. Clear intake paths, practitioner profiles, search-ready structure, and ongoing web management."
  additionalSchema={[serviceSchema]}
>
  <section class="page-intro">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">Website design for therapists & counselors</p>

      <h1>Custom website design for therapists and counseling practices.</h1>

      <p class="page-lead">
        Brighter Sites designs custom websites for therapists, counselors,
        psychologists, clinical social workers, and small group practices.
        The goal is not just to make the practice look professional. The site
        should make it easier for prospective clients to understand who you
        help, how you work, and what they should do next.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section tint">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">Built around the client decision</p>
        <h2>People looking for therapy are not shopping for an ordinary service.</h2>

        <p>
          A prospective client may be comparing clinicians while already feeling
          overwhelmed, uncertain, or unsure whether reaching out is the right
          decision. The website needs to communicate trust and professionalism
          without making important information difficult to find.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="feature-grid">
        <article class="feature">
          <h3>Help people understand fit</h3>
          <p>
            Your specialties, approach, clinician information, and practice
            personality should be clear enough that someone can decide whether
            it makes sense to learn more.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="feature">
          <h3>Make services easy to understand</h3>
          <p>
            Individual therapy, couples work, trauma treatment, anxiety support,
            EMDR, telehealth, and other services need enough structure that
            visitors can find the information relevant to them.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="feature">
          <h3>Give people a clear next step</h3>
          <p>
            Appointment requests, consultation forms, phone calls, email, and
            client-portal links should be easy to locate without overwhelming
            the rest of the site.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container service-detail-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Practice-specific structure</p>
        <h2>The website should match the way your practice actually operates.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="detail-list">
        <div>
          <h3>Solo and group practices</h3>
          <p>
            A solo clinician may need a focused personal presentation, while a
            group practice may need clinician profiles, multiple specialties,
            and clear pathways to the right provider.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3>Services and specialties</h3>
          <p>
            Dedicated service pages can explain the problems you help with,
            treatment approaches you use, and who each service may be a good fit
            for without forcing everything onto one long page.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3>Practical practice information</h3>
          <p>
            Telehealth availability, office information, insurance or payment
            details, frequently asked questions, and contact expectations can be
            organized so visitors know what to expect.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3>Privacy-conscious presentation</h3>
          <p>
            Forms, external scheduling systems, client portals, analytics, and
            third-party tools should be considered carefully when they are part
            of a behavioral-health website.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section tint">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">Practice systems & secure pathways</p>
        <h2>Connect your therapy website to the systems clients and referral sources already use.</h2>
        <p>
          A behavioral-health website often sits in front of scheduling, intake,
          client-portal, and referral systems. Brighter Sites can create clear
          pathways into supported workflows from platforms such as SimplePractice,
          TherapyNotes, and Alma without pretending the public marketing site
          should replace those systems.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="feature-grid">
        <article class="feature">
          <h3>SimplePractice website pathways</h3>
          <p>
            Direct prospective clients to supported consultation, contact,
            scheduling, or client-portal destinations while keeping the public
            website focused on education, fit, and trust.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="feature">
          <h3>TherapyNotes intake and portal pathways</h3>
          <p>
            Build clear calls to action around the practice's supported
            TherapyNotes workflows instead of collecting sensitive clinical
            information in ordinary website forms.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="feature">
          <h3>Alma profile and referral pathways</h3>
          <p>
            Connect appropriate public profile or referral destinations when
            Alma is part of how the practice receives inquiries or presents
            insurance-network information.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <div class="ownership-note" style="margin-top:2rem">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">HIPAA-conscious website decisions</p>
          <h3>Keep marketing pages and sensitive client workflows in the right places.</h3>
        </div>
        <p>
          Brighter Sites does not label an ordinary public website “HIPAA
          compliant” as a blanket promise. Forms, analytics, scheduling links,
          portals, and third-party scripts need to be evaluated based on what
          information they handle. When sensitive information is involved, the
          website should route people into an appropriate secure system rather
          than collect more data than the marketing site needs.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">Search visibility</p>
        <h2>A strong therapy website also needs a structure search engines can understand.</h2>

        <p>
          Brighter Sites builds the technical foundation for useful page titles,
          service-focused content, clean URLs, mobile performance, search-engine
          indexing, and local-search signals appropriate to the areas your
          practice actually serves.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="feature-grid">
        <article class="feature">
          <h3>Service-focused pages</h3>
          <p>
            Important specialties and services can have their own useful pages
            instead of being buried inside a generic services list.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="feature">
          <h3>Technical SEO foundations</h3>
          <p>
            Metadata, canonical URLs, sitemap structure, crawlability, responsive
            design, and performance are handled as part of the build.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="feature">
          <h3>Measurement after launch</h3>
          <p>
            Search and website data can help identify which pages are gaining
            visibility, what visitors use, and where future improvements may be
            worthwhile.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <p style="margin-top:2rem">
        <a class="text-link" href="/website-strategy/">
          Explore SEO and analytics for therapy websites →
        </a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="cta-panel">
        <p class="eyebrow">Build around your practice</p>
        <h2>Your website should feel like your practice—not like a therapy template.</h2>

        <p>
          Tell me about the clients you serve, the services you offer, and what
          you want someone to understand when they first find your practice.
          We can turn that into a clear website plan before you commit to the
          project.
        </p>

        <a class="button" href="/contact/">Plan a custom therapy practice website</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
